\section{CRS handling}{

Functions that compute metric quantities on geographic coordinates
(\code{\link[=spat_area]{spat_area()}}, \code{\link[=spat_distance]{spat_distance()}}, \code{\link[=spat_overlap]{spat_overlap()}}) use the geodesic
measurement capabilities provided by \link[sf:sf]{sf} (via s2).
An optional projected CRS may be supplied via the \code{crs} argument when planar
computation is desired.

In contrast, \code{\link[=spat_length]{spat_length()}} strictly requires a projected (planar) CRS
(either on the input data or supplied via \code{crs}) because planar coordinates
are required for line clipping and length measurement.
}

\section{Geometry repair}{

When \code{repair = TRUE} (the default), invalid geometries are repaired on
internal copies used for computation.
The geometry returned to the user is always the original target geometry.
